# Cursor Rules for DAM Frontend Project

## Code Quality Rules

### 1. Props Interface Rules
- All interface properties MUST be readonly
- Use `readonly` modifier for all props in React components
- Example: `readonly title: string;` instead of `title: string;`

### 2. Code Complexity Rules
- Maximum cognitive complexity: 15
- Maximum cyclomatic complexity: 15
- Refactor functions that exceed these limits
- Extract helper functions to reduce complexity

### 3. TypeScript Rules
- Use strict TypeScript configuration
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use readonly arrays: `readonly string[]` instead of `string[]`
- Use readonly objects: `readonly { key: value }` instead of `{ key: value }`

### 4. React Component Rules
- Use forwardRef for components that need ref forwarding
- Use useImperativeHandle for exposing methods via refs
- Prefer functional components over class components
- Use proper TypeScript generics for component props

### 5. Model Organization Rules
- Define all interfaces in `/src/models/` folder
- Group related interfaces in the same file
- Use descriptive file names (e.g., `QueryModels.ts`, `UserModels.ts`)
- Export interfaces from models folder
- Import models using `@models/` alias

### 6. Import Rules
- Use absolute imports with aliases (`@models/`, `@common/`, `@pages/`)
- Group imports: React imports first, then third-party, then local
- Use named exports for interfaces and types

### 7. Function Rules
- Maximum function length: 50 lines
- Extract complex logic into separate functions
- Use descriptive function names
- Prefer pure functions when possible

### 8. Error Handling Rules
- Always handle errors in async operations
- Use proper error types and messages
- Log errors appropriately
- Provide user-friendly error messages

### 9. Performance Rules
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Use useMemo and useCallback for expensive calculations
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Use proper dependency arrays in hooks

### 10. Accessibility Rules
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Provide proper ARIA labels
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Use proper color contrast ratios

## File Structure Rules

### Models Folder Structure
```
src/models/
├── QueryModels.ts          # Query-related interfaces
├── UserModels.ts           # User-related interfaces  
├── AssetModels.ts          # Asset-related interfaces
├── DatabaseSchema.ts       # Database schema interfaces
└── index.ts               # Re-exports all models
```

### Component Structure
```
src/common/components/
├── ComponentName/
│   ├── index.tsx          # Main component
│   ├── types.ts           # Component-specific types (if needed)
│   └── utils.ts           # Component-specific utilities (if needed)
```

## Examples

### Good Interface Definition
```typescript
// src/models/QueryModels.ts
export interface QueryResult {
  readonly headers: readonly string[];
  readonly data: readonly Record<string, any>[];
  readonly query: string;
}

export interface QueryResponse {
  readonly totalQueries: number;
  readonly results: readonly QueryResult[];
}
```

### Good Component Props
```typescript
interface ComponentProps {
  readonly title: string;
  readonly data: readonly DataItem[];
  readonly onAction: (id: string) => void;
}
```

### Good Function Complexity
```typescript
// Good - Simple function
const formatDate = (date: Date): string => {
  return date.toISOString();
};

// Good - Complex logic extracted
const processUserData = (users: readonly User[]): readonly ProcessedUser[] => {
  return users.map(processUser);
};

const processUser = (user: User): ProcessedUser => {
  // Complex processing logic here
  return {
    id: user.id,
    name: user.name.toUpperCase(),
    // ... other processing
  };
};
```

## Enforcement

These rules should be enforced through:
1. ESLint configuration
2. TypeScript strict mode
3. Code review process
4. Automated testing
5. Cursor AI assistance

